n DSA problem categories. Organizing the DSA content into a structured learning path requires categorizing problems into existing topics such as trees, tries, heaps, backtracking, graphs, shortest path algorithms, and 1-D dynamic programming. This categorization will allow for a more intuitive and progressive learning experience. The categorized topics will be represented in the DSA learning dashboard.\n\n\nExpanding the DSA curriculum necessitates a comprehensive problem set across diverse categories: dynamic programming (1D, 2D), greedy algorithms, interval-based problems, math/geometry, and bit manipulation. Verifying the problem counts across identified topics (Arrays & Hashing, Two Pointers, etc.) shows it's a 300-problem set.\n\n\nProblem categorization is refined; the problem count verification aligns with the defined structure. A critical check confirms compliance with problem specifications, including unique IDs, LeetCode identifiers, canonical titles, difficulty levels, topics, DSA patterns, problem descriptions, and runnable Python starter code with test invocations. The total number of problems is confirmed to be 300 to match the requirement.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/engine.js\"","EndLine":"60","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Inspecting engine.js header and architecture\"","toolSummary":"\"engine.js architecture check\""}}]}
{"step_index":1761,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T06:13:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-27T11:43:10+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-10-27T11:43:10+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/engine.js`\nTotal Lines: 2916\nTotal Bytes: 100543\nShowing lines 1 to 60\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: // app_build/engine.js — Universal Offline Python Execution & Memory Visualizer Engine\n2: // Zero AI APIs. Supports:\n3: // - Full OOP: Classes, `__init__`, `self`, instance attributes (`self.items`), instance methods (`s.push(10)`, `self.is_empty()`)\n4: // - Full DSA & Standard Python: Lists, Dicts, Sets, Strings, Slicing, Comprehensions, Recursion, Loops, Short-Circuit (`and`/`or`)\n5: // - Thonny Visual Reference Model (`Name -> @Address -> Value`), Scope Isolation (`Global` vs. `Local`), and Visual GC\n6: \n7: const PRELOADED_PROGRAMS = {\n8:   max_subarray: {\n9:     title: \"Maximum Subarray (Kadane's Algorithm)\",\n10:     defaultInput: \"nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\",\n11:     algorithmOverview:\n12:       \"This code finds the largest sum you can get from a continuous section of the list.\",\n13:     code: `class Solution:\n14:     def maxSubArray(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\n15:         c = 0\n16:         ans = nums[0]\n17: \n18:         for i in range(len(nums)):\n19:             c += nums[i]\n20: \n21:             if c >= ans:\n22:                 ans = c\n23: \n24:             if c < 0:\n25:                 c = 0\n26: \n27:         return ans`\n28:   },\n29:   stack_class: {\n30:     title: \"Stack Data Structure (OOP Class & Methods)\",\n31:     defaultInput: \"\",\n32:     algorithmOverview:\n33:       \"This code defines a Stack data structure (Last-In, First-Out) using a Python class and tests push, pop, peek, size, and is_empty.\",\n34:     code: `class Stack:\n35:     def __init__(self):\n36:         self.items = []\n37: \n38:     def push(self, item):\n39:         self.items.append(item)\n40: \n41:     def pop(self):\n42:         if not self.is_empty():\n43:             return self.items.pop()\n44:         return None\n45: \n46:     def peek(self):\n47:         if not self.is_empty():\n48:             return self.items[-1]\n49:         return None\n50: \n51:     def is_empty(self):\n52:         return len(self.items) == 0\n53: \n54:     def size(self):\n55:         return len(self.items)\n56: \n57: \n58: # Example usage\n59: s = Stack()\n60: s.push(10)\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
