d, each showing the solved/total ratio with respective progress bars. A prominent \"Continue Learning\" card will suggest continuing a recent problem or, if none, recommend a beginner problem with a \"Start Problem\" button for immediate visualization. A recent\n<truncated 9402 bytes>\nnd \"notes\" fields.\n\n\nInitializes the beginner roadmap with eight structured phases, each detailing learning objectives and relevant problems. The roadmap phases cover topics from fundamental data structures to advanced algorithms and will offer \"Solve\" actions for interactive learning. A progress display will indicate completed problems within each phase.\n\n\nA dashboard structure is proposed, organizing the content into header statistics, progress cards for topics and difficulties, and beginner roadmap with a problem explorer. The main layout encompasses a stats strip, \"Continue Learning\" and \"Recently Practiced\" cards, a grid of topic cards, a beginner roadmap timeline, and a curated problem library. The library will provide search, filter, and sorting functionalities for the problem catalog.\n\n\nClicking the \"Solve & Visualize in Python\" button triggers a view switch to the code visualizer. The code editor is updated with the problem's Python code and the top bar text. Problem data is saved in local storage. The `app.runUserCode()` function executes, initiating the visualization process. Returning to the DSA library is handled through specific UI elements, refreshing the dashboard progress.\n\n\nThe core problem library (`dsa_problems.js`) containing 300 curated DSA problems will be created with detailed metadata per problem. The `dsa_dashboard.js` module will manage the dashboard's internal state, integrate local storage for persistent user progress, and implement dashboard functionality including roadmap rendering and problem interaction.\n\nThe `index.html` file will be updated to include the necessary JavaScript files and add a view toggle between the visualizer 